From: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw89: 8832br: limit the memory of the TX queues

mac80211 lets the TX queues of a device hold 16 MiB when a band supports
VHT, and 4 MiB otherwise. The RTL8832BR sits in routers: on a Cudy WR1500
(128 MiB of RAM) 20-30 MiB are free while it runs. When TX to the
stations can't keep up with what arrives from the other interfaces, the
TX queues fill up to their limit, and all that memory stays with them
for as long as the backlog lasts. Most of it is receive buffers of the
Ethernet driver, which then has to allocate new ones while memory runs
out.

Set the memory limit of the RTL8832BR to the 4 MiB mac80211 gives HT
devices, as mt7996 sets its own. That still lasts about 30 ms at the
highest PHY rate of the chip, and it can be changed at run time
("iw phy <phy> set txq memory_limit <bytes>").

Signed-off-by: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
---
diff --git drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/core.c drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/core.c
index 94fbcdf..e4f6fb5 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/core.c
@@ -7474,6 +7474,15 @@ static int rtw89_core_register_hw(struct rtw89_dev *rtwdev)
 	hw->wiphy->tid_config_support.peer |= BIT(NL80211_TID_CONFIG_ATTR_AMSDU_CTRL);
 	hw->wiphy->max_remain_on_channel_duration = 1000;
 
+	/*
+	 * The RTL8832BR is found in routers with little memory, where the
+	 * 16 MiB that mac80211 lets the TX queues of a VHT/HE device hold is
+	 * much of what is free. The 4 MiB of HT devices still last about
+	 * 30 ms at the highest PHY rate of the chip.
+	 */
+	if (rtw89_is_rtl8832br(rtwdev))
+		hw->wiphy->txq_memory_limit = 4 << 20;
+
 	wiphy_ext_feature_set(hw->wiphy, NL80211_EXT_FEATURE_CAN_REPLACE_PTK0);
 	wiphy_ext_feature_set(hw->wiphy, NL80211_EXT_FEATURE_SCAN_RANDOM_SN);
 	wiphy_ext_feature_set(hw->wiphy, NL80211_EXT_FEATURE_SET_SCAN_DWELL);
